<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sirhind</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sirhind-Fatehgarh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sirhind is a historic town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, which was the seat of a Mughal governorship controlling the plains south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a>. Its governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wazir">Wazir</a> Khan executed the two youngest sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a> in 1704, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-singh-bahadur">Banda Singh Bahadur</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> forces sacked it in 1710. Its final capture by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-confederacy">Sikh confederacies</a> in 1764 opened the region to the Phulkian and other Sikh states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sirhind.jpg" alt="Sirhind" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kuldeepburjbhalaike · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gurdwara_Fatehgarh_Sahib.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
